+When a stage needs a value from the user's shell environment, never start an interactive shell (`zsh -i`, `bash -i`, `zsh -ic '…'`) to read it: the interactive shell takes the terminal's foreground and does not hand it back, so the wizard is suspended by SIGTTIN (`zsh: suspended (tty input)`) at its next prompt. First use the value the wizard inherited (`${VAR:-}`), secret or not. Otherwise, never `source` or `eval` a shell file or line, and take a non-secret value only as the literal text of the `export VAR=…` line in `~/.zshrc` (or `~/.bashrc`), with its surrounding quotes removed; when that text contains `$` or a backtick, `ask` for the value instead. Take a secret from the platform's secret store: the macOS Keychain (`security find-generic-password -s <service> -w`), or, where a keyring is running on Linux or WSL, `secret-tool lookup <attribute> <value>`. When none of these yields the value, `ask` or `ask_secret` for it. Pass each value to another program as a `VAR=value cmd` prefix, never as a command-line argument, where `ps` would show it.
+
 Hold the bar the template sets: open the URL before asking for its value, use `ask_secret` for anything secret, `write_env` every persisted value, `set_secret` only the values CI actually needs, and `confirm` before any irreversible action. Each `stage` clears the screen so only the current step is visible: keep a stage to one focused task so nothing the human needs scrolls away. Don't touch the library above the marker.
 
